[Music]  hello everyone and welcome to today's  webinar oncotopicsdiscovery  deep learning as a standard analysis  method for digital pathology images  i am marie stone of labyrinths and i'll  be your moderator for today's event  today's educational web seminar is  presented by lab roots and brought to  you by vizio farm to learn more visit  viziofarm.com  we encourage you to participate today by  submitting any questions you may have  during the presentation  to do so simply type them into the ask a  question box and click send  we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for at the end of the  presentation  you may also submit any technical issues  here as well if you have trouble seeing  or hearing the presentation  i'd like to now welcome our speakers dr  fabian schneider r d and product  management at physio farm  and dr david mason senior technical  sales specialist at vizio farm  dr schneider dr mason you may now begin  your presentation  welcome to this physiopharm presentation  introducing oncotopics discovery  a quick disclaimer all products  mentioned in this presentation offer  research use only  vizio farm does have a portfolio of  clinically appropriate products if  you're interested in learning more  please use the email address on the  screen now  within the field of digital pathology  and digital image analysis  there are many common questions that  people have  when approached with complex problems  here are some of them  there are so many packages available so  many software and so many approaches  that many people are confused about  which one is best or which one is  appropriate for their particular system  or model  we also hear a lot of people who are  domain experts in their biological area  unsure how to convert their biological  knowledge into analysis  lastly many people who approach an image  analysis workflow find that there's too  much time spent in development training  or modifying existing workflows  this combined with many other questions  leads us to the point that there's a  need for powerful tools but that are  easy to use and accessible to everybody  hopefully during the course of this  short talk we will answer some of these  questions and demonstrate how vizio  farms approach can be used to solve  these and many other problems  i'd like to start by addressing this  first point  visio farm has been in this digital  pathology and digital image analysis  space for over 20 years  during this time we've seen many  different  approaches  and we are of the opinion that deep  learning has now got to a point where  there is the optimal approach for the  vast majority of questions within the  digital pathology space  complex problems require advanced tools  and this is where deep learning shines  you can see in this example  this is a tumor detection problem in  breast cancer  you can identify tumor areas by their  darker hematoxylin or darker blue  staining such as the area identified  here  and you'll notice that the contrast  between these structures and stromal  structures  are very low  it's a really subtle difference in  nuclear shape  and also morphology  this is also a particularly difficult  problem because as you can see  in this ihc image  some of the cells are positive for dab  this brown stain and some are negative  and in this case we can of course see a  large variance of the number of positive  cells and their morphology when we look  at different sections  deep learning allows you to segment  these complex tissues without the need  for secondary or complex stains  often using simple stains such as h e or  ihcdab  it handles subtle or low contrast  structures very well  and lastly possibly the most important  point is that deep learning allows you  to build robust approaches that can  handle stain and tissue variants such as  the ones that are commonly seen with  different section thicknesses different  staining modalities or different  approaches  these are key points as to why we feel  deep learning is the best approach in  the vast majority of biological  questions  but more than that what this enables  is to allow the software to take over  and replace laborious manual tasks such  as identifying areas of interest or  excluding artifacts with end-to-end  automation  now you don't have to take our word for  it  a simple search of the literature of  papers published in peer-reviewed  journals  shows that the field is definitely  moving towards adoption of deep learning  pathology and we believe will continue  to do so as these technologies are made  more available to more people  so what sort of problems can deep  learning solve in the digital pathology  space  the answer is almost everything that  we've come across  from simple things such as nuclear  detection in bright field and  fluorescent images  tissue segmentation to identify areas of  necrosis or metastasis  and even outside of oncology  into toxicological pathology looking at  things like kidney structure glomerular  detection  cell invasion and cell classification  deep learning has almost infinite scope  as to the sorts of projects stains and  approaches that it can help with  having explained what deep learning can  do  what i'd like to point out is that with  visiofarm's release of the new  oncotopics discovery platform we have  decided to take this technology and give  it to everyone who accesses vizier farm  software  we call this deep learning for everyone  as we truly believe that this  groundbreaking technology of deep  learning  can be used to solve a vast range of  problems  this means that anyone engaging with  visiofarm will then have access to deep  learning tools in the standard package  this means using these powerful tools  across any modality  imaging mass cytometry fluorescence or  bright field imaging for example  and all of the major file formats  of course the standard package comes  with fully customizable outputs so you  can create the metrics that you desire  it's accessible with no coding or  scripting required  and it also has extensible functionality  and what i mean by that is that for  instance if you use tissue microarrays  you can use our tissue arraying tool to  easily de-array and manage tma cores for  individual analysis  likewise if your work involves high or  ultraplex data as we often see in  fluorescent imaging or imaging mass  cytometry you can use our specialized  multiplex phenotyping and data  exploration tools  to help your studies using these data  types  if you ever do serial sections or serial  staining approaches  regardless of the modality bright field  fluorescence or a combination of both  we have tissue align tools that within  the software register these serially  sectioned or serially stained images  these three packages are fully  integrated with the visiofarm software  so you can use them in combination with  our deep learning tools that come as  standard  we also have a range of extended  authoring tools that let users optimize  deep learning settings and pick from a  wider range of classifiers  i'd like to now talk about how we can  take these deep learning tools and use  them to actually solve problems in a  method that we call see it train it find  it  this really addresses the problem of  taking your biological knowledge and  converting it into analysis  we've taken these tools that are highly  powerful and basically made them easy to  use without any computational knowledge  whatsoever  in the first step the user simply looks  at their images  and identifies different areas that  they're interested in  segmenting in this case you can see  tumor and stroma highlighted  the second step is simply to provide  annotations  giving examples of tumor areas here in  red and stromal areas here in green  with a push of a button the user can  then train the software to show what  these different areas look like across  single or multiple images  once this is done we can move on to step  three which is find it  taking a whole slide image and then  simply running the app that has been  produced  then provides you with any number of  results in both regions or numbers that  you'd like  see it train it find us  the last thing i'd like to mention is  the pre-trained knowledge that comes  with oncotopics discovery  as we've mentioned it's very easy for  users to develop their own apps with the  see it train it find it approach  but in many cases there's even a quicker  way to get to your answers  visio farm can be used across  any range of tissues stains and  modalities  and oncotopics discovery deep learning  is versatile and robust  so what this means is that not only can  solutions be used  to take into account  the variability seen across different  images  it can also handle different modalities  such as fluorescence  imaging mass cytometry and brightfield  and this pre-trained knowledge is  available to end users with a push  button simplicity  a great example of this is with our  nuclear detection apps  available to users of visiofarm that can  just be plugged straight into your own  data  and the majority of cases will work off  the shelf  this significantly speeds up development  time and allows you to  get to your results much faster than  training these yourself  i'd like to now give a quick example  demonstration of some of the pre-trained  knowledge and how it looks in the vizio  farm software  here we can see a whole face breast  cancer section  stained with ihc dab for ki 67 a  proliferation marker  if we zoom into the image we can see  different areas of interest and we have  some tumor areas and some stroma areas  as introduced earlier in the talk  visio farm runs well across whole slide  images but can also be used on regions  of interest within images  it's worth noting that regions of  interest can be adapted  erased  or added  to identify specific regions of interest  here by selecting a region and then  simply hitting run  from the ribbon  we can start to use this pre-trained  knowledge to identify  tumor areas within this region  you'll notice that in real time here the  tumor areas are identified and outlined  in this blue dashed region of interest  we also have access to customized  metrics such as the tumor area in  millimeter squared  one of the really powerful things about  this pre-trained knowledge is that you  can actually daisy chain together  multiple apps here not only to detect  tumor but for instance to do a cell  detection and classification  which can be specified to be within  tumor regions within stromal regions  or both  this is particularly useful if you've  done tissue segmentation for instance to  exclude areas of artifacts  these then are no longer analyzed as  part of your whole sequence saving you  time and providing an end-to-end  analysis solution  as you can see the cells are detected  classified and then recorded  each of these cells is counted as either  negative in blue or positive in red the  numbers are reported in the results  table  and also a custom metric the  proliferation index is calculated this  is simply the percentage of positive  cells  here the overlays opacity can be changed  and customized to see the underlying  data  and confirm that these are in fact  identifying and classifying cells  correctly  this is a great example of pre-trained  knowledge in action  using off-the-shelf apps to do tumor  detection and cell detection  i'd like to highlight another example of  where pre-trained knowledge can be used  and also the power of deep learning  approaches  here if we look at  kidney images  where the aim of the project is to  identify glomeruli  what we can see is that in all of these  cases the same app has been run  to detect glomeruli regardless of their  underlying staining  on the left is cd68  in the middle is collagen  and on the right is nephron  this highlights the power of deep  learning where you can identify  the same structures in three different  staining regimes that are drastically  different  simply by seeing it training it and  finding it  users can create their own powerful deep  learning apps to solve these sorts of  problems  i'd like to thank you for your attention  for this presentation of oncotopic's  discovery  our approach of deep learning for  everyone  see it train it find it  and the demonstration of pre-trained  knowledge  if you have any questions after this  presentation  you can use the email address shown on  this slide  thank you dr schneider dr mason for your  informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  let's get started  our first question is  how is the vizio farm platform installed  workstation server cloud and ce etc  thank you very much marie for the  question um  i would like to hand this to david as he  is one of our  senior technical sales specialist to  answer that in detail okay  hi thank you for the question um vizio  farm can actually be installed in any  number of these environments so  oftentimes people will match the  underlying hardware to their required  turnaround time for instance if you  wanted to run a 1000 slide study  very quickly in half an hour or an hour  for instance then we can certainly  advise on how you would want to install  into a gpu cluster for instance it's  worth pointing out also that if you are  installing on physical workstations or  workstations in the cloud that we also  have options for parallelizing  processing  so for instance you can utilize as many  computers as you have to split up the  processing jobs and the training jobs  and  make it a much more efficient processing  thank you  next question  do i have any control of the deep  learning settings  yes you have so in the in the standard  version you don't  then you can just  add your annotations and labels as  needed for the training of the deep  learning  but with the  extension of your platform with the  extended author you also have a module  to tweak  the parameters of the networks  thank you  our next question is  is an h e train algorithm also good for  an ihc image  yes this is a good question and it  definitely depends on which  um  part of the agent you have trained on so  if this would be for example  a nuclear  with  the um  hdx sustaining detection then you are  able to transfer that but our platform  comes with pre-trained uh nuclear  detection networks as indicated by  david's talk maybe do you want to add  something to this  yeah i think i would just add that this  is one of the real benefits of deep  learning is that the output will highly  depend on the input so for instance if  as with the kidney case that i showed in  the talk you had an h e image and an ihc  image you can include training data from  both of those different stains  and that way it allows the app to  understand  what the thing you're looking for is  even if the staining regime changes it's  incredibly powerful in that regard and  as  fabian mentioned the one example that we  have that ships with every vizio farm  install is the cell detection that works  out of the box across h e and ihc  great thank you  um  next question  does see it train it find it work in  fluorescent data what is the maximum  number of input channels i can use  that's a great question  david  yeah so actually there's no practical  limit of the number of input channels  and something not mentioned or mentioned  briefly in the talk is that visio farm  is actually acquisition agnostic so we  frequently work with both ihc data  with fluorescent data in the low plex  six to seven channels  with fluorescent data in the  intermediate high and ultraplex as well  as imaging mass cytometry  so the some of the biggest data sets  we've seen are actually 70 or 80  channels or even more but there's  actually no limit on the input data  you also have the choice as to which  channels are included in the see it  train it find it approach with deep  learning  yes and just to add on that um  with our platform you also get hands on  all the native file format reading like  we are the only platform natively  reading uh the fluidim mcd file formats  with all metadata we are  reading the qp tiffs from akoya and can  stitch all of these images together  automatically upon import  we read  nanostring images we read 10x genomics  images we can read also  annotations from ai fourier we can read  past core image file formats etc  so with with this platform you have a  great  abundance of image file formats that you  can easily use  great thank you  our next question is  how can end-to-end analysis be performed  if there are bubbles or tissue folds in  the images  that's yeah this is a great question and  i'm happy to take this one um  obviously what we're trying to do with  deep learning is enable people to do end  to end as the questioner asked and  really by removing the need to manually  exclude these artifactual areas it gives  you an incredible power to be able to  batch process large amounts of data  and the answer is actually in the see it  train it find it approach because if you  have for instance a tissue section and  you'd like to exclude all of the tissue  folds  dust staining artifacts you can simply  highlight these areas in exactly the  same way that we highlighted the  different areas in the tumor example  and then choose which areas of the  tissue excluding the artifacts you would  like to go on and process  so it really gives you a very powerful  approach to be able to end to end  analyze  even quite complex images  yes and i think what we need to add to  this is  if you can  see it and label it then you can create  your networks to perform your qc  of all your images for exclusional  great thank you  next question  if i want to perform my own analysis can  i access the underlying data  um  i don't know what  to to fully i don't fully understand  that question david you  yeah i mean i can take a stab in this  case oftentimes what we hear people  wanting to do is you know if you have a  particular workflow and you have a  favorite statistical approach downstream  of your analysis  then you may want to take that and then  run your data through an r script or a  custom visualization for instance  and at vizio farm we're not in the  business of locking people into the  platform so you can always access both  the data the numerical outputs from your  analysis but also the masks as well so  any of the regions of interest that  you've seen in the talk today  can also be exported either as tiffs or  as xml if you prefer structured data so  if that's what the questioner was asking  then you can be assured that you can  always export your results and export  your overlays and then customize how you  use them downstream  yeah  maybe another  take on how you can how i understood the  question as well is  yes all the pre-trained  networks you can adjust to your own  needs and can train further on it if it  doesn't perform on your  tissue type for example  thank you  next question  how do you do qc on this  accuracy and precision of the analysis  that's a good question and i think  what you have to do is uh  best explained by david  yeah sure thing and you know qc is an  absolutely critical part of any image  analysis development  and in this case what we come across is  what are you comparing to so visio farm  is a very easy to use interface if you  want to bring a pathologist in and just  by eye look at the outputs and confirm  whether or not they fit what the biology  is suggesting to the biological expert  you can do that of course in some cases  you won't have direct access to the  pathologist so for instance you would  have a ground truth set of data or a  ground truth set of masks these can be  easily imported into the vizio farm  platform and you can actually calculate  inline  sensitivity and specificity scores  if you wanted to look at overlap of  course there are many metrics you can  calculate such as the dice index or the  the jacquard index and this tells you  how well your app is performing compared  to your ground truth  of course one of the biggest  restrictions is where do you get that  ground truth from and we've run into  something called the the gold standard  paradox of course is that you're always  comparing to something else  one other thing to add is when you are  doing qc with visio farm when you run an  app you can always lock it down and  suggest that you don't want any more  changes to the way that this deep  learning app works  or if you're during development you can  review the results and then use a form  of transfer learning where you simply  correct the outputs and then hit  continue training and continue to refine  your deep learning application  so it's really flexible about how you  approach it but we give you the tools  and the platform to be able to qc and to  be able to calculate these sensitivity  scores  thank you  we have time for one more question does  the software score the biopsy tissues  such as the gleason scoring in case of  prostate cancer  yes so that's a great question so  this is already going into the  translational more clinical side and the  oncotopics discovery is our research  platform  and we also have an oncotopics clinical  platform in which we have ivdr or  certified um  apps like for ks67 and breast tissue or  her2  and these are now  further developed in the future and what  you can always do and this is the beauty  of the system is that you can  create any outputs for your um for your  networks that you would like to have  so you have a vast majority of  post-processing steps and then can  create a vast  amount of outputs like  all the intensities  positionings and then give  different cutoffs of your intensities a  different score  yeah and i would just add to that also  that in many cases if people are taking  the first steps into digital pathology  and digital image analysis we can  replicate many of the scoring techniques  that people are used to doing at a  microscope so for instance selecting 10  high power fields and then scoring  within those or randomly assigning  regions to to analyze and coming up with  more qualitative scores that match how  the pathologist may be assessing broadly  some of the tissues so flexibility is  really the key in how we approach  outputs  thank you again uh what we have one more  question we have time for one more  question does the software provide  contrast enhancement for visualizing the  tissues  so there's a full suite of visualization  options  in visio farm so you can always control  for instance with multiplex data which  channels are represented which channels  are shown in which combinations change  their individual colors but also alter  the brightness and contrast and some  gamma settings either broadly for the  whole tissue or per channel so on a  specific case  this is really critical in some cases if  we do  multiplex where some of the stains are  very dim and some of them are very  bright obviously you would need to do a  per channel  adjustment so that you can visualize the  structures that you're trying to train  and  analyze  thank you  just one more question can it detect  mitotic figures  um yes so  if you can train it on the mitotic  figures then you can definitely detect  metallic figures the question is you can  also do the transfer learning like if  you have a histone a phosphoric stone h3  stain  and you can  um  align the slide with an agent e then you  have exact uh presence of where you have  your  mitotic cells on the uh on one cell on  the on slide one compared to slide two  and you can transfer that and pre  perform the algorithms for example  um the the deep learning training of  with this information from the phosphory  stone h3 you can also do this with  sequential staining like  you first stain for the agent e wash it  and then do the take a picture and then  you do the phosphorus tone h3  i would also add to that that if you're  interested in abnormal mitoses we can  also just using the same see it train it  find this approach in a simple h e image  for instance  identifying not only cells that are  non-mitotically active and mitotically  active but identify various stages or  abnormalities for instance cells  dividing incorrectly or getting stalled  halfway so  the the trick is to try to provide  flexibility in a way that allows you to  answer your biological questions  and it's important that you can annotate  all of these different stages so that  you have enough examples for the for the  network to learn it  well thank you again dr schneider dr  mason for your time today and your  important research we would also like to  thank lab roots and our sponsor vizio  farm for underwriting today's  educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event  until next time goodbye  you

